<x-layout title="AI & Human Engineering Judgment | The Approach | Project 407" description="Why an experienced engineer matters alongside AI. Project 407 combines AI-assisted development with human judgment, practical testing, and responsibility for software in production.">
    <div class="studio-page">
        <section class="studio-intro"><div class="studio-intro__line"><span>THE APPROACH / HUMAN IN THE LOOP</span><span>AI EMBRACED · EXPERIENCE APPLIED</span></div><h1>AI is powerful.<br>Software is still <em>complex.</em></h1><div class="studio-intro__foot"><a href="#judgment">GO BEYOND THE DEMO ↓</a><p>Getting an idea onto a screen is more accessible than ever. Turning it into software your business can depend on takes context, engineering, and someone accountable for the result.</p></div></section>
        <section id="judgment" class="studio-section"><div class="studio-split">
            <div><p class="studio-kicker">WHY INVOLVE AN ENGINEER?</p><h2 class="studio-heading">A working demo is a useful beginning.</h2></div>
            <div><p class="studio-lead">Building with AI yourself can be a great way to explore an idea, learn what you need, and test an early workflow. If you have already started, bring what you have. The next step is understanding what is ready, what needs attention, and what is worth building further.</p><p class="studio-lead mt-8">Real users introduce questions a demo can leave unanswered. Who can access each record? What happens when an integration fails? Can data be recovered? Will the system remain understandable as it changes? An experienced engineer knows to ask those questions and can work through the answers with you.</p></div>
        </div></section>
        <section class="studio-section studio-section--muted"><p class="studio-kicker">WHAT THE HUMAN ADDS</p><div class="studio-proof">
            <article><span>01 / CONTEXT</span><h3>Build the right thing.</h3><p>Question assumptions, understand users, and decide whether to build, buy, connect, or simplify before adding more software.</p></article>
            <article><span>02 / ENGINEERING</span><h3>Make it dependable.</h3><p>Review architecture and data access, test important behavior, and handle failure paths alongside the happy path.</p></article>
            <article><span>03 / OWNERSHIP</span><h3>Think past launch.</h3><p>Plan for deployment, monitoring, recovery, maintenance, and the next person who will need to understand the system.</p></article>
        </div></section>
        <section class="studio-section studio-section--dark"><div class="studio-split">
            <div><p class="studio-kicker">HOW THE WORK HAPPENS</p><h2 class="studio-heading">Use AI deliberately. Keep judgment close.</h2></div>
            <ol class="studio-list">
                <li><span>01</span><strong>Agree on the outcome</strong><p>Understand the current process, constraints, and what improvement would be valuable. Define how we will assess the result.</p></li>
                <li><span>02</span><strong>Choose the smallest useful step</strong><p>Make the scope and tradeoffs clear. A focused review, a prototype, an integration, or a roadmap may be the best starting point.</p></li>
                <li><span>03</span><strong>Build with AI and review with care</strong><p>Use AI where it helps exploration and implementation. Review generated work, verify key behavior, and make deliberate decisions about sensitive information and tool access.</p></li>
                <li><span>04</span><strong>Deliver with a plan for what follows</strong><p>Check the result against the agreed goal, explain limitations, and establish ownership and the next steps for support or continued development.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </div></section>
        <section class="studio-section"><div class="studio-split"><div><p class="studio-kicker">LESS WORK FOR PEOPLE</p><h2 class="studio-heading">Let software handle the routine.</h2></div><div><p class="studio-lead">The aim is to remove unnecessary tasks, decisions, and handoffs. Use existing systems and channels wherever practical. Keep people involved for approvals, judgment, and exceptions, with clear controls and a way to review what happened.</p><p class="studio-lead mt-8">For a customer-facing product, that means an experience that helps people accomplish something. For an internal system, it can mean fewer logins, fewer status checks, and a useful notification only when someone needs to act.</p></div></div></section>
